#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120595} :activity/id #uuid "9a213bfe-4551-5c56-be1e-798baa94efb6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048339 :topic/id #uuid "96c0d2ef-1891-5083-879c-8dcc09b70542" :topic/math-academy-id 1834} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121008 :tutorial/content "The **osculating circle** (sometimes called the *kissing circle*) of a curve $C$ at a point $P$ is a circle that\n\n- is tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P,$ and\n\n- has the same curvature as the curve $C$ at the point $P.$\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve $C$ given by the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t+1, 2-t^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ and its osculating circle at the point $P$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f51c1d1f45f2484991c28296d9b9ceeda134965993d130c2611c0e4bfb4ab32b.png)\n\nAt the point $P(1,2),$ where $t=0,$ the curvature is $\\kappa(0)=2.$\n\nIt can be proved that any circle has constant curvature, which is equal to the reciprocal of the circle's radius. So, for a general curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ we define its \n **radius of curvature** as\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa (t)}. % = \\dfrac{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\right\\|}{\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\|}\n$$\n\nIn our example above, we get\n\n$$\nR(0) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa (0)} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Since computing the radius of curvature requires computing the curvature first, we might use different formulas for curvature depending on the situation.\n\nFor example, if a curve is parametrized by its arc length, we use the formula\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa (s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\| \\mathbf{T}'(s) \\|}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, if a curve uses a general parameter $t,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa (t)} = \\dfrac{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\right\\|}{\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\|}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b58176ee-322d-5b4a-aa8c-e9972cbe1229" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "812b9e66-1124-5116-9356-ca760a8dde07" :step/math-academy-id 11116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119574 :question/id #uuid "3e36e916-16af-55eb-9ece-026b68eb93ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3338"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049196}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492066}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"} :step/id #uuid "869f0a40-96aa-5ddd-b227-5006ff45ea4d" :step/math-academy-id 11118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121009 :tutorial/content "The center of the osculating circle for a curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at a point where $t=t_0$ is located at the tip of the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t_0)+R(t_0) \\mathbf N(t_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf N(t)$ denotes the principal normal vector to the curve.\n\nFor example, the curve $C$ parameterized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\bigg\\langle t+1, 2- \\dfrac{t^2}{2} \\bigg\\rangle\n$$\n\nhas the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{N}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1+t^2}} \\langle -t, \\: -1 \\rangle ,\n$$\n\nand the radius of curvature at the point $P(1,2)$ where $t=0$ is $R(0)=1.$\n\nSo, the center of the osculating circle is at the tip of the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0+1,\\,2-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}⟩+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}}}⟨0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2⟩+⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ is located at $\\left(1, 1 \\right),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8cd8407e6398b3409e7f575e152976669393914e2e5d7335121287276af6e41.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a31a99b-6c09-5d1c-ad68-7b5f38c17efb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2489 :tutorial/title "The Center of the Osculating Circle"} :step/id #uuid "88a27337-45d9-59ab-ac93-024d63adfb66" :step/math-academy-id 11120 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119575 :question/id #uuid "7f4861fe-a783-5874-a22b-b4644257ae0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3339"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048616}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492055}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"} :step/id #uuid "4edcd617-8c81-5a66-b0c0-b9510d42e399" :step/math-academy-id 11121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121010 :tutorial/content "If a curve has a curvature of zero at some point, the radius of curvature at that point is defined to be infinite.\n\nIt follows from the definition that a straight line has zero curvature at all of its points. Conversely, if a curve has zero curvature at any point, it must be contained on a straight line.\n\nSuppose now that we want to find the radius of curvature for the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t^2, \\: 1 - t^2, \\: 1+ t^2 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nat an arbitrary point where $t> 0.$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we used the fact that $\\sqrt{t^2}=t$ since $t>0.$\n\nNext, we find the unit tangent and its derivative for $t>0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}t}\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(t) & =⟨0,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}t}{0}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, despite its \"parabolic\" appearance, the given curve is contained on a straight line!\n\n**Note:** Often, the unit tangent vector of a curve $\\mathbf T(t)$ may look a bit complicated. In such cases, the following formula involving a cross product may be useful. It says that the radius of curvature of a twice-differentiable curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nR(t)=  \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} =\\frac{{{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}^3}}}{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right) \\times \\mathbf r''\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "590485ba-30a6-549b-a4cf-d6641c92a033" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2490 :tutorial/title "Infinite Radius of Curvature"} :step/id #uuid "5b0a8082-2fe6-5852-b950-a17b2bc3d3ca" :step/math-academy-id 11122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119576 :question/id #uuid "8d58f131-7260-5346-9a88-60862c0a1b55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3340"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048648}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478445}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"} :step/id #uuid "25d03bd3-ad37-5232-b295-cdb7c4d45fa8" :step/math-academy-id 11123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120599}}] :activity/title "Radius of Curvature" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120034}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478469} :activity/id #uuid "721b1290-aefc-5e37-b344-d5131a4a3506" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048339 :topic/id #uuid "96c0d2ef-1891-5083-879c-8dcc09b70542" :topic/math-academy-id 1834} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0589c295-d29a-5525-8df9-84a2fef5c818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360850} {:answer/id #uuid "07bef1ff-2469-5d35-b897-08a2224bc1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360863} {:answer/id #uuid "4e594d6b-dcde-5053-8f5b-521e676bfdea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361178} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7bf037-7a41-5080-8e72-6de80d6a14df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,-1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361463} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a6e881-f847-56c1-9261-cac4148a3edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360863} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d378b87-11c8-5248-9b4e-02b0168b1e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24945e7c-95e1-50bc-b79f-c2e7d2556ce8" :question/math-academy-id "q-97593" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0)$ where $t=0$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t),$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,0⟩,$ since the point $P(1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,0⟩+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02094b1d-f4cb-5c2d-b251-9b08dea3ddda" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58d19bde-c557-5f9c-8b97-9ee98b0b44b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨0,1⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263763} {:answer/id #uuid "8d133154-399b-5788-8d63-d8c5a7d9ea07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264207} {:answer/id #uuid "975c77d4-38ea-5a25-aa07-13a8a2dc63a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-1,0⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264300} {:answer/id #uuid "b5763ceb-8908-51fe-ab60-358749e774f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨0,-1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264569} {:answer/id #uuid "edaf1b77-f55a-53de-aaf6-870a334b4eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $⟨-1,0⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265077} :answer-field/id #uuid "61507bf1-0dd4-5fab-a0ea-a38287bd4d32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6665004f-e6b2-5e6c-b764-778c8eb359b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132201" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,1⟩,$ since the point $P(0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,1⟩+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9eccffe8-ee2f-59ee-9cc6-9c221f7ead6a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e7b0c5-59c8-5276-8f27-e519196c22e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263236} {:answer/id #uuid "5c697a54-9e06-52cc-9c8e-9e93cd4a2158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263796} {:answer/id #uuid "a532f5f7-12c2-5842-a3e6-eb768839607c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264413} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6b8756-6f83-5bd9-aa3b-ae5978576099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264473} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8a8631-251a-5a00-aa65-16be619ec141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264630}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264630} :answer-field/id #uuid "805bbad1-9820-5da9-8726-919fd6256468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d2943df-d322-57f9-9465-3be5dfbf7067" :question/math-academy-id "q-132129" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-\\pi }⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi }⟨1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & ={e}^{-\\pi }\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =2{e}^{-\\pi }⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =2{e}^{-\\pi }⟨0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-\\pi }⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times 2{e}^{-\\pi }⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2\\pi }\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2\\pi }\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2\\pi }k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2{e}^{-2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{-\\pi }\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{2{e}^{-2\\pi }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-3\\pi }}{2{e}^{-2\\pi }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{-\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b1e1ea61-7fee-5b07-820f-6ba30cb6f254" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ea7b209-5616-55d5-9904-552d1c0715cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477851} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff08d81-960b-574d-bb5a-4735a65941c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477931} {:answer/id #uuid "623c10d1-ccda-5676-a53a-28fffcd4fe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477937} {:answer/id #uuid "6d62eea6-536d-5d3a-b711-a9c0a42702d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477961} {:answer/id #uuid "729b116e-a1cd-54d1-af16-82f64c0e08d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477851} :answer-field/id #uuid "3901cec7-c587-5a5c-8a5e-3e53db5169e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bee1b915-a838-5542-8a64-106e980bc53d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98666" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=1$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(1)$ and ${r}^{″}(1)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d82a98fc-1a8f-5783-a491-1003f56ccece" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478498}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0c45fb-1a1d-5177-bf4c-fd01be65faa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263096} {:answer/id #uuid "39c3dbdf-5f6f-5fe6-bac2-7cf9c7f67e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263514} {:answer/id #uuid "4350ceb2-104a-5954-9cd1-0a02d74f4a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263594} {:answer/id #uuid "9ccf270e-4167-5b66-a2b9-51b0179a8785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264341} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2ae9ed-c588-5454-885d-f17ba33f35b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263594} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef9e59a-6517-5986-94c0-f586c58a7db0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "258f49b8-8aac-592e-a98b-1492e2d635c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-132122" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨0,-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fca31df7-2371-581c-a00e-69db776a5a38" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478486}}] :activity/title "Radius of Curvature" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477729}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492091} :activity/id #uuid "3e9ef5eb-888c-5cbc-84df-f6df39a4af07" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048339 :topic/id #uuid "96c0d2ef-1891-5083-879c-8dcc09b70542" :topic/math-academy-id 1834} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfef024-1b6c-555f-8a26-33bf987ce229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8d1fdb-1e8d-5f02-9ea7-c3c298ae432f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361248} {:answer/id #uuid "af862e42-0cba-5667-86e1-bcba2137990c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361614} {:answer/id #uuid "c253a7fe-6f5f-512c-aff1-bf5aa8dd058e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361708} {:answer/id #uuid "ed37a445-de09-58c0-b587-33b13984c10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361889}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9e95f8-2048-5fb9-b5b3-a7a136bdb4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "61a53a3d-9f1a-5b06-bcde-4575632a0617" :question/math-academy-id "q-98665" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3},6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\,-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}{\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "360756d1-180d-59b9-adc1-f4b18b0a5d95"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c981d0-879c-5395-961f-18d30c7ee1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491467} {:answer/id #uuid "30ccc38c-3c4b-51d7-a8be-015493817d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491550} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9407b6-9b59-518b-866e-5aec8e95de1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{33}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491798} {:answer/id #uuid "cbceddc2-2b0f-51bf-9732-a4901abca037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491799} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c914c6-693e-5352-8b50-6792d0a0a2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491872}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491467} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d0d86a-f951-5330-9b83-6ded6a5ca5d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c0a58b0a-c208-5232-9cb9-1df706de5400" :question/math-academy-id "q-34618" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $r(0)$ if the curve and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{2t},\\,t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{(2{e}^{2t}+1)^{2}}⟨{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{3t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,-2{e}^{2t}⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{2t},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${T}^{′}(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{2}{(2{e}^{0}+1)^{2}}⟨{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0},\\,-2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{9}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{9}=\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $r(0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{3}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "421db3cf-202e-5ae2-a58a-3d2eeca55e71" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492101}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2870427b-9fcb-5fd7-b0fd-1a1a1b0dc4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491538} {:answer/id #uuid "353d2804-a43e-5df9-80ef-d3844280008d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491559} {:answer/id #uuid "7e064b2d-d342-5924-8c38-37c0f7e7f14a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491676} {:answer/id #uuid "97265fa4-83a5-5552-82a3-d03e500d17ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491713} {:answer/id #uuid "a67f422f-ea71-5192-afa8-1cf02f8737c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491745} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c46275-3046-5183-a66a-80853ae0abe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e6c5544d-5a76-5194-9e05-adccd06c8464" :question/math-academy-id "q-36223" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$,  given that the curve and its unit tangent $T(t)$ are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,1⟩,\\,T(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of the osculating circle: \n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the radius of the osculating circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9c6ec06b-19b9-5029-9158-da22d4a86ae0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff6569a-4cab-5e8f-8762-376041784c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-2)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360893} {:answer/id #uuid "35e1a2f8-2f8a-5d39-9327-ca2e3e8e0ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361077} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed54666-3b56-576a-9615-df8106a5c984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361398} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa0a898-43f7-561b-bd46-61b1497e96c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,-1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361406} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ad7825-cc9e-5adb-aafe-2969fcf48b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(\\pi ,1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360893} :answer-field/id #uuid "b90568e4-8415-56af-8bbd-a969ac099004" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4602817c-a855-5ccd-8d1a-bca1a25a4651" :question/math-academy-id "q-97594" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨\\pi ,2⟩$,  since the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,-1⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩+4⟨0,-1⟩=⟨\\pi ,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d21bdd95-0820-5dce-89ad-01f22a360825" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1060224c-d0b3-5776-a766-96b15404205d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(2,0)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491485} {:answer/id #uuid "28349558-5db2-5c09-8b29-f853f31f0913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(2,0)$ and the radius is $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491537} {:answer/id #uuid "31280857-9970-5bf7-888c-fc39c357541b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(2,2)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491551} {:answer/id #uuid "5611b291-95d5-535d-999e-5155c671c2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(-2,0)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491619} {:answer/id #uuid "910409ee-6695-5f67-a309-77a3fe4d5eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The center is $(0,2)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491485} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa7d73e-6b11-5043-84cc-72ac6b11b133" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb82f4d9-80ff-57cb-8e48-0da61beda250" :question/math-academy-id "q-97585" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(2,2)$ where $t=2$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}}{2},\\,\\frac{2-t}{2}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(2)=⟨2,2⟩,$ since the point $P(2,2)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(2)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(2)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2-t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2-t}{2\\sqrt{4t-{t}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =⟨\\frac{2-2}{2\\sqrt{4(2)-{2}^{2}}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(2) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=2$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2)+R(2)N(2) & =⟨2,2⟩+2⟨0,-1⟩=⟨2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e0ee45a0-d850-5a33-afdc-ce8ffcb1cb89" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492107}}] :activity/title "Radius of Curvature" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491443}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125440} :activity/id #uuid "9faeb18a-39f2-528b-927a-5e59d0f90fc7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048582 :topic/id #uuid "ab43842c-a68a-5473-977a-14bce3aa134e" :topic/math-academy-id 1967} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125440 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125799 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose we have the linear map\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: {\\color{blue}V} \\to {\\color{red}W}\n$$\n\nwhere the bases of the vector spaces $\\color{blue}V$ and ${\\color{red}W}$ respectively are given by\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}=\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_1},{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_2}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_3} \\}, \\qquad \\qquad {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = \\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{c}_1},{\\color{red}\\mathbf{c}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nThe **matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$** is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}({\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_1}),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{b}_2}),$ and $\\mathbf{T}({\\color{blue}\\mathbf{b}_3})$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{B|C} & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ [T({b}_{1})]_{C} & [T({b}_{2})]_{C} & [T({b}_{3})]_{C} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, if \n\n![](images/be/be8cff88ef83ebc67d0df7a50a243c55c8f11aa92e7a07f64b73bb30ed701392.svg)\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{B|C} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 10 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da91373e-0973-56a4-91c9-aa2a2b1de0b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3589 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9ee24e02-1f78-5b25-8d1c-f19b4a0052ee" :step/math-academy-id 20152 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125441 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124542 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124351 :question/id #uuid "e9539818-ad55-5bb3-8c5d-5b85303f9e6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5521"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84947238-f446-5261-8ecd-ee263f64e04f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050252}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549937}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Linear Map Given the Matrix Relative to Two Bases"} :step/id #uuid "d4cf88b7-d61d-5b12-a8f6-f786898cb8c3" :step/math-academy-id 20154 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125442}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125442 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124543 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124352 :question/id #uuid "8a371b65-8c57-5d10-88aa-2d4928fb028c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5522"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8725caed-481c-5fbe-a0c4-cffdcb308881" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480712}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Given With Respect to the Standard Basis Under a Linear Map"} :step/id #uuid "80774b4b-be41-5637-b9a9-f728e8911827" :step/math-academy-id 20155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124603 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124415 :question/id #uuid "01b46867-ba6d-5085-9543-b77ded581194" :question/math-academy-id "e-8604"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f1e542-f0a2-54ea-ba7f-0a9928415907" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050065}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501158}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Map Relative to Two Bases Using the Definition"} :step/id #uuid "a48f8724-cd55-576f-8431-f47c3d90338a" :step/math-academy-id 31941 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124604 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124416 :question/id #uuid "3d6b0aec-c282-581e-ae4a-560654d80e3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8605"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0587fad6-5ef2-5200-9b60-ddbd0bc21ba7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501135}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Map Relative to Two Bases Given One Basis"} :step/id #uuid "f3a9b6e8-c0c7-5e91-a3fa-8081e91b886a" :step/math-academy-id 31942 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125843 :tutorial/content "Given the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}|\\mathcal{C}}$ of a linear map $\\mathbf{T}:V \\to W$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C},$ and the coordinates $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B},$ the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ can be found as \n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{C}} = \n[\\mathbf{T}]_{{\\mathcal{B}}|{\\mathcal{C}}}\\cdot [\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n![](images/e3/e3623ffb88602004ad3ab01f55976d3a55759563a6088f4e2a10b7b8849a6f70.svg)\n\nthen the image of $\\mathbf x$ under $\\mathbf T$ relative to $\\mathcal C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "db73b6f4-55d4-5d30-856e-463bc6cc9964" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5093 :tutorial/title "The Image of a Vector Under a Linear Map Given by a Matrix Relative to Two Bases"} :step/id #uuid "14e61c95-f43b-5c3d-8e38-75c4db0838d1" :step/math-academy-id 31944 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125441}}] :activity/title "The Matrix of a Linear Map Relative to Two Bases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124752}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501244} :activity/id #uuid "151e5f91-3cb4-5ced-a9bd-b1b2446d0e25" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048582 :topic/id #uuid "ab43842c-a68a-5473-977a-14bce3aa134e" :topic/math-academy-id 1967} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501244 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394529b7-5240-5ff7-90db-e1add45b32a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500582} {:answer/id #uuid "825811a4-08b5-5ffb-990f-7e501d2498b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500736} {:answer/id #uuid "9efa7096-ec71-5ba8-a546-d69b56f674bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500789} {:answer/id #uuid "aad3aeef-b512-5754-9b36-a0105a67e7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500820} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f6fe27-6dff-5e8f-81fc-28e28ad0e3ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500736} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b795f03-1c4c-51ce-9ec3-51380c3631d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3a497a8c-181f-5ddb-8e0c-d7f0dcc66829" :question/math-academy-id "q-38861" :question/problem "Suppose $T:V\\to W$ is a linear map and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ are the bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. Find the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T$ relative to these bases, if \n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=-3{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2},\\,$ $T({b}_{2})={c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{3})=5{c}_{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the images of vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1})=-3{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{1})]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2})={c}_{1}-4{c}_{2} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{3})=5{c}_{1} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{3})]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2})$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0408ea5d-a169-5ec2-b15a-fbb75152c5cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501248}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3811dc15-f044-5933-86e0-b81332887411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500581} {:answer/id #uuid "575b1543-5913-5111-9e9c-4cf207869497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500645} {:answer/id #uuid "5e277c6c-dcd2-5491-b1f3-c7fa6def4dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500660} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c8e6c-8875-5f05-83c5-ba640b983ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500687} {:answer/id #uuid "be36abcb-60b4-5b59-8d60-819c63d7b780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500862} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4374709-dd94-564d-a093-c34e0f6d470b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00e54e6a-cd0f-51b4-97b3-2489c07dd2c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97574" :question/problem "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b3a2889-b1be-57f0-91ef-41940e81fc0b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501265}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4176c859-5854-5080-bf2e-b0f94f0b8361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361127} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb06e3c-e23d-5e91-bbec-4e481c417779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361321} {:answer/id #uuid "7eaadcc6-81a8-5407-bdd2-2c06f604c335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361396} {:answer/id #uuid "a69c20d3-4c5a-587d-8103-f2c720627c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361584} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ce3e71-5c81-5e9f-a3e1-5fabb47b6eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361801} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eea433c-814e-5e0e-919d-f13b06399a26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c64bcdb5-768e-5293-a151-ad4d8fbb8250" :question/math-academy-id "q-97579" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "236b321d-1506-5f60-8283-c253a6c03092" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501276}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501265 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4984b8-1073-51f4-bbe6-0c45bb082a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500491} {:answer/id #uuid "1601e7df-ddae-5494-a41c-da35e76fa3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500516} {:answer/id #uuid "83748e6b-9023-5628-b878-282bfb4de93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500739} {:answer/id #uuid "97e4106e-ea27-5370-8a2c-fa0b2b2c526d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500777} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7cb8ed-bc84-5b04-a0d8-47c0b7dd5e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500491} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b8b066b-e90d-5c38-b2a6-4d8b3185f0d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba29e43e-8fe2-5bbe-a64e-366d83a4fca9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97578" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "467923da-fcad-50c7-8110-cff41e775129" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501257}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336fb41f-4416-5b90-9963-63b715d894cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361063} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7b5d9e-e2af-5819-9157-eb95f22a5fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361315} {:answer/id #uuid "860742e3-e187-5777-a59d-99f7335da169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361436} {:answer/id #uuid "d4184a13-5037-5335-bef6-cc89b6e2f44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361788} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c815cc-4d5f-5de4-92b1-bdce9f7a0197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361873} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e0a7bfb-89f3-51f0-b3f7-efaec681dd46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "29ca0842-b657-584e-9ec2-fc1fbb7af353" :question/math-academy-id "q-97584" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B|C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} &=-4 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7e3e85bd-ebee-5a59-b335-848da96d3843"}] :activity/title "The Matrix of a Linear Map Relative to Two Bases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500459}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193493} :activity/id #uuid "555c23df-016c-5847-bcc4-15704704dfb4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801 :topic/id #uuid "14fbe17c-9f56-5dba-963a-a64e379ba05f" :topic/math-academy-id 4012} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193814 :tutorial/content "The **expected value** (also known as the **expectation** or **mean**) of a continuous random variable is the average value we'd expect to get if we observed the random variable many times.\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the continuous random variable $X$ with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x}{4}, \\quad 1 < x < 3\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "727440ce-9bc9-544c-889f-00c2107d4977" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4804 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "49df91fa-7474-5cd7-bce7-3052e981b2e4" :step/math-academy-id 29818 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192617 :question/id #uuid "61c51284-18f0-5398-857b-7687228e8df8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7985"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3da4448-71cc-5671-8f32-a9f293ad5054" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425377}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Continuous Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "d5f5c529-09c9-5bd8-8f07-58cf47f6162e" :step/math-academy-id 29819 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193496}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192618 :question/id #uuid "c755eddf-3b0d-5a33-aa13-e6a3f7da4244" :question/math-academy-id "e-7986"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8322a1ba-7608-53de-88d9-e360ca8025cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549946}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Continuous Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "f67c50e7-8c03-5eaf-85c4-84bd537eb4d0" :step/math-academy-id 29820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192619 :question/id #uuid "3a11b9e3-60c8-57f3-8ec6-4ff378d0db62" :question/math-academy-id "e-7987"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b84b8fb-5ad7-5c4f-af44-b6d1dc9310c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050215}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436280}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Continuous Random Variable With a Piecewise PDF"} :step/id #uuid "8ce5d9b1-f0eb-5bc1-9dae-be9875f2c61f" :step/math-academy-id 29821 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193495}}] :activity/title "Expected Values of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192901}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436284} :activity/id #uuid "ef1cf134-d58c-50a9-859d-b58621f65432" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049801 :topic/id #uuid "14fbe17c-9f56-5dba-963a-a64e379ba05f" :topic/math-academy-id 4012} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436284 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b4f1c9-6a5b-52c7-aa2b-f725b7d61594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436256} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd6386a-9b90-5c4e-be0a-bdee4537678d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436261} {:answer/id #uuid "6b109b1d-9413-505f-ad96-a041eb6ca00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436265} {:answer/id #uuid "e329b197-e48b-55a0-8164-4bc46b1e5644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436273} {:answer/id #uuid "e4dba7f7-2146-5ff3-a626-ce33b04b8a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436265} :answer-field/id #uuid "aded4a40-1d18-5fec-bc4b-4bcb36ab2d42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26e02fce-e08e-5fe6-a3ec-6ad998577401" :question/math-academy-id "q-90448" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Give your answer rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-1, & 2<x<3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{12}, & 3\\le x<4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{5}{12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{5x}{12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{2}^{3}+{\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{24}|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{16}+\\frac{35}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{145}{48} \\\\  & ≈3.02,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "07e26220-f667-5519-be8e-7ecfe5527804" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436285}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436285 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201c2488-3b9d-5108-a48b-2c3d64cef328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425362} {:answer/id #uuid "637130b5-f7d6-51c6-b45d-c736d39b3a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2e+3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425364} {:answer/id #uuid "70653d2a-2e94-5850-abe7-6d163b8005a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2e-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425366} {:answer/id #uuid "814d0b3f-29a5-5f01-b16e-f12a0f9ff292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425367} {:answer/id #uuid "f493205a-4bbe-5f60-96f9-afe578c94f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425366} :answer-field/id #uuid "b56cdda3-d5c2-5ae0-8fb8-26559a6d5135" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e9404e17-b865-57fd-909a-78dee18906a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130871" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & 0<x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2x}, & 1\\le x<e\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{0}^{e}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}+{\\frac{x}{2}|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{e}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e-1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4a878e04-e7e6-5c4d-a22c-de06d29307c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436288}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436286 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12065d32-e113-5992-bb8d-f535e75fb9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436257} {:answer/id #uuid "18efb766-1d3d-58da-bff5-d24f02292777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436258} {:answer/id #uuid "29173ece-c32d-5596-a3a8-34d0349c05ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436259} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a8b51a-6b08-55ab-89b7-ef577ff59874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436268} {:answer/id #uuid "b73c6eb0-be0e-57a4-8c3b-4e4f51c6378c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436257} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d69cbc-7ea6-5219-b9fe-b27e60e2aa63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c64c8376-4f9a-5df0-81b9-bf811d12f7ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-152904" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[4X+2].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of the expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[4X+2]=4\\text{E}[X]+2\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8})|}_{0}^{1}+{(-\\frac{1}{2x})|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{8}-0)+(0-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4X+2] & =4\\text{E}[X]+2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{7}{8}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5cb7073d-7ea9-5c29-94c4-88d377c15dab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30945d0b-c228-50cd-9399-4fc6759027b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436260} {:answer/id #uuid "477a366f-5027-5734-8066-1c5b19a5ae4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436262} {:answer/id #uuid "52eacba8-2ac3-5a25-85fe-9c675a072c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436263} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffc82d5-d3b0-5643-95db-cbc049b36c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436264} {:answer/id #uuid "c10ec2db-f24e-557d-8a16-961d2cfcd677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436260} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9112695-ad08-5300-a06c-ea0b770acb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "edac9b93-8284-5302-bc8b-9583d4ecc8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-152907" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[12X-8].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{8}{9}, & 1\\le x<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of the expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[12X-8]=12\\text{E}[X]-8\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{8}{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8x}{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12})|}_{0}^{1}+{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{12}-0)+(\\frac{16}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[12X-8] & =12\\text{E}[X]-8 \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{17}{12}-8 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "95f58f9a-4473-5f86-8f32-f1873a14a714"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77138f44-7002-5bb8-ac6c-cc59e24ef477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436266} {:answer/id #uuid "990d8356-79d6-5c6b-8d72-fd39fd724f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436267} {:answer/id #uuid "c85412a1-0e83-564f-8a9b-83a321c6575e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436271} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fa3a08-2b65-5156-aae1-9ccac58678b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436272} {:answer/id #uuid "e8914a7c-ea0f-5a3e-bdf9-4afc9ee04ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436271} :answer-field/id #uuid "40bc13bb-3a56-5bb3-8230-e9e5cf644479" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3743ef7f-fa52-55af-8a67-99557ea6f27d" :question/math-academy-id "q-131154" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[2e-(e-1)X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{e}{e{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}},\\,1<x<e\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2e-(e-1)X]=2e-(e-1)\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate the expected value of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{S}x\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\cdot \\frac{e}{e{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{e}{ex-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{e-1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{e}{e-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{e-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2e-(e-1)X] & =2e-(e-1)\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =2e-(e-1)\\cdot \\frac{e}{e-1} \\\\  & =2e-e \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d8cd80ec-e90e-5ae1-afb0-eecf1d561a37" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436286}}] :activity/title "Expected Values of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436255}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120652} :activity/id #uuid "acb4d472-a6fc-5c2c-a30f-1918865acaa4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050129 :topic/id #uuid "2eee4710-3f26-5186-948b-a819cfaf4083" :topic/math-academy-id 1775} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119788 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119599 :question/id #uuid "4efedc4e-19e9-552c-bc27-814df953e141" :question/math-academy-id "e-4575"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fcf3704-4856-5095-a6f4-7ec20d81f3c4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441148}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing an Unknown in Terms of 2x2 Determinants"} :step/id #uuid "8cb786bc-aa30-57b8-aa52-e09a7018f11b" :step/math-academy-id 16704 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119601 :question/id #uuid "311ba12f-de22-580c-9e0d-6766f7864d1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4578"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441149}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "0691d00f-77e7-57d5-b71b-b1153b0b4afa" :step/math-academy-id 16710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121027 :tutorial/content "**Cramer's Rule** is a method for solving a linear system of equations using determinants.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y &={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y &={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/c7/c7ef883a1d4dd8c1fc0fc8fbde5ed80c9a5229650fc77c77559b71552370c35b.svg) Then Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\nx =  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{D_y}{D}\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n![](images/b1/b13ecd10395fe0977d04d627179667cbca207fcf4015a89d43df0e69765f1f5e.svg)\n\nFor example, let's use Cramer's rule to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2y &=-1 \\\\ -1x+2y &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the **determinant of the coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $D_x$ and $D_y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{4}{4}=1, \\\\ y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{8}{4}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is common to write the solutions to a linear system as a vector. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y \\right> = \\left< 1,2 \\right>.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3adc5c1c-58e5-5c0a-a8f7-da6abd6f06e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3102 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "48887938-d6a0-5822-b084-3bf10a3a4461" :step/math-academy-id 16830 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120731}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119658 :question/id #uuid "d81e32a1-bd93-5a95-bb4a-e8df9063b5aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5230"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7580024-f357-57d4-94f2-85c25e2c144b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550021}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parts of Cramer's Rule Formula"} :step/id #uuid "720a6f93-380c-5e70-8c81-e99f44a4ef11" :step/math-academy-id 19262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120647}}] :activity/title "Cramer's Rule for 2x2 Systems of Linear Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119997}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088643} :activity/id #uuid "c7c1a0ce-38f8-537d-a8f0-5c255830b86e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433 :topic/id #uuid "48b31687-d86d-5039-a373-e5b296daf525" :topic/math-academy-id 879} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088941 :tutorial/content "When the characteristic equation of a second-order homogeneous ODE has a **repeated root** $\\lambda = c,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{cx} + Bxe^{cx}.\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's solve the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y =0.\n$$\n\nAssuming a solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ we differentiate and substitute into the ODE:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+9{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{\\lambda x}$ is never zero, we can divide by it to get the **characteristic equation**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 3$ is a **repeated root**. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a4fc9c29-f54a-5346-b7b3-59abdf8bb39f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4049 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0a076b74-a850-5e47-b647-e51c36c1259a" :step/math-academy-id 24226 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087592 :question/id #uuid "81531c55-fe2e-5eff-82ba-9e08abf24a0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5fb4d6b-2c86-5ad6-898b-5a1cd44e4106" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423299}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation With Repeated Roots"} :step/id #uuid "f1046776-23ad-5801-b3b5-f777cbc55e3b" :step/math-academy-id 24228 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087593 :question/id #uuid "3363cf38-10a2-5a55-9451-ee2101b8e809" :question/math-academy-id "e-6592"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebe4460-e57e-546f-99c8-81192aafc1ec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423300}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions When the Characteristic Equation Has Repeated Roots"} :step/id #uuid "e78e8b59-e0a4-5f68-8156-3b7d5844fde6" :step/math-academy-id 24229 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087594 :question/id #uuid "abc842b6-8b86-585c-a425-5776909c6425" :question/math-academy-id "e-6593"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ad20bb-c6a4-5390-a11a-e5277cfd1667" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444008}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "8392881d-182b-5602-83c1-1fbe45b22882" :step/math-academy-id 24230}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087944}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444009} :activity/id #uuid "d70c6a0e-1f3c-563a-8c55-406c4c3e138f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433 :topic/id #uuid "48b31687-d86d-5039-a373-e5b296daf525" :topic/math-academy-id 879} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b85ad48-9f95-542b-b9e7-333573122a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345999} {:answer/id #uuid "5df5ae30-aca2-5b8b-a7ef-ab9a0e07d1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346157} {:answer/id #uuid "87ddb655-179d-58ca-aee8-81d2759013c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{5x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346529} {:answer/id #uuid "a30734ea-8cc5-594e-99de-f5929b08b6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346763} {:answer/id #uuid "ade2ac4a-023e-51b4-86dd-4276ec9f1a4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{-5x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346763} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddfd4a05-11a3-55b9-a24a-46a074d48938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a3297f16-33bd-57f9-a38b-3244518aab25" :question/math-academy-id "q-7796" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{25}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +\\frac{25}{4}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +\\frac{25}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -\\frac{5}{2})(\\lambda -\\frac{5}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0f286fd7-1908-5288-96d6-dcf89110d360" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444013}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d8a3c5-5363-5440-81b4-736a6ed048c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263316} {:answer/id #uuid "4be3230f-287e-5bc5-bb8a-0bc800ebb580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263653} {:answer/id #uuid "54d9255b-1d3e-556a-8e47-22f8f0859221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263729} {:answer/id #uuid "95061659-d6cf-5b2a-ac7e-6647bf34f7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264276} {:answer/id #uuid "acb55b46-a13b-5b43-98b6-e37cd7cfa6e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263316} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a73307-ba25-593c-b49a-b3c51f5baaf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd7e4f6c-a1fc-5a7a-9880-7a238668b459" :question/math-academy-id "q-130138" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "12f24fc0-6891-5b1a-9924-a75b1a7ee50c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c7eef7-3d78-5989-86e0-94ecf6198c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263025} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6f9528-c212-5abe-9e54-8bfc43b253d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264323} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c75313-b7a0-5910-aede-49a9ecc39bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264524} {:answer/id #uuid "b4adbff7-3555-5242-bfca-c5d264d3ce55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264553} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a21b02-ca2e-51b8-92c2-391a79250846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264524} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4383e0a-d8be-5d10-8611-d4c6afc8cf56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "94dec214-fc61-500b-bffe-c52024a2504b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130147" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-2x}$ and $x{e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution."} :step/id #uuid "3d0273da-7dca-5b4d-9e87-7ca443d6f6e9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19665675-933f-562b-92c2-453f3aa6a7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x{e}^{-x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443996} {:answer/id #uuid "1c7e410e-ff0e-513f-b439-59db85b21e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{-x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443997} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc60826-5099-595c-bb90-6813ebbf7d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{e}^{-x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443998} {:answer/id #uuid "59ac1266-2808-5904-a986-8f81e5f69ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{-x/4}-x{e}^{-x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443999} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7c927a-01c3-5ee9-9a6b-b90fcf3476e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{-4x}+3x{e}^{-x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444000}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444000} :answer-field/id #uuid "47117b4f-de1f-5179-8ffa-cfd17b335562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5dbc9673-510f-532c-a004-54ed22aabfa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64796" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{16}y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda +\\frac{1}{16}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda +\\frac{1}{16} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\lambda +\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{4}$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-x/4}$ and $x{e}^{-x/4}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{-4x}+3x{e}^{-x/4}$ is not a solution."} :step/id #uuid "4a132e70-8467-55e5-b1db-1bc17136f384" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05281020-031d-5d2a-808e-01688d4b6930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{9t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443995} {:answer/id #uuid "9983f332-f05f-500a-8acb-70984c9621d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t{e}^{9t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444001} {:answer/id #uuid "c635f663-c965-5072-9ef5-15319fccaa59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{9t}+5t{e}^{9t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444002} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f68714-c0ba-58d7-8203-728b2921b006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{-9t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444003} {:answer/id #uuid "e69ea800-ad27-5d19-8fda-f9c165e154aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7t{e}^{9t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444003} :answer-field/id #uuid "585f5d02-4d6c-51a6-9fd9-0f3602ee74e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e9b8a76-fadd-5c5e-88d1-453c1509beac" :question/math-academy-id "q-64795" :question/problem "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-18{y}^{′}+81y=0$ for $y=y(t),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-18\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+81{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +81) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +81 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -9)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =9$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{9t}$ and $t{e}^{9t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $3{e}^{-9t}$ is not a solution."} :step/id #uuid "6a42458d-290e-54dd-b688-d7fd5bd9fa4a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444012}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443994}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963} :activity/id #uuid "0e238e7b-3085-5b87-b3c4-0f5c35258e51" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820 :topic/id #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" :topic/math-academy-id 1977} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130347 :tutorial/content "Consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n![](images/4f/4fd591d8028847aa7e1d11e840acdb192703d152f9c397e40fec98fd1e281c41.svg)\n\nIf we are told that one of the eigenvalues of this matrix is $\\lambda = 3,$ how do we calculate the corresponding eigenvector?\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this reduced matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_3 = 0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/70/705dd92c7330897f35671b66866791160b9d7f65e3280d5ccb9d992405c08942.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/2a/2a5dd6c35fb81134f13e765e0a02be4ea2feedfd878a1923491813492739ef00.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b1a9d148-15f7-5878-8f36-f19ee99c49d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3566 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "875dc5aa-e4f4-50f8-a30e-d5ed46d77e32" :step/math-academy-id 20099 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129127 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128938 :question/id #uuid "d6c73b7b-4981-5908-b694-be7f0e57b612" :question/math-academy-id "e-5490"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455512}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "59537f37-7060-5544-9b4a-84501e450c50" :step/math-academy-id 20100 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129128 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128939 :question/id #uuid "b087e601-c054-563c-b7ee-43e18c74ac51" :question/math-academy-id "e-5491"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431011}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "8a926ac2-afd2-5b57-8911-c3b8dac86d39" :step/math-academy-id 20101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129129 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128940 :question/id #uuid "c22ab531-3377-5827-93d2-a13854b3308c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538983}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5d1fcdf4-d676-5ff5-bf31-d7e371f43f08" :step/math-academy-id 20102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130403 :tutorial/content "Note the following important theorem.\n\n*The set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_k \\}$ of a matrix $A,$ where each vector $\\mathbf v_i$ corresponds to a different eigenvalue, is linearly independent.*\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can* be (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "c0f0f6d3-c9e1-52a6-bf51-244bb7751a9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4432 :tutorial/title "Eigenvectors That Correspond to Different Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "3314b023-f15c-5f8b-a4e4-aeea702377a2" :step/math-academy-id 27265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129325}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477000} :activity/id #uuid "3ab8d004-5a46-5aac-836a-cb6aba195154" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820 :topic/id #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" :topic/math-academy-id 1977} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5988b748-db6c-5b36-9840-e6478cabbb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321853} {:answer/id #uuid "7c472c58-699c-5782-89ce-b06e067314de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322125} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7fab-ed01-5f57-aecb-974f3540bbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} {:answer/id #uuid "d0690c0d-0bea-5716-9be4-2e7a0d6ed043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322774} {:answer/id #uuid "dc30c442-053c-588a-a4c2-b47c4d9a660e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb178049-21d1-5f35-85d8-2469af417f05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8f655022-c86d-5349-9391-8c2ff380546e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37403" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{20}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2(4{x}_{3})-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "55c470c3-9a2c-5b79-a7cb-6bb99638869c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "963f3713-419f-5a42-a8fd-36820ed2b9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399243} {:answer/id #uuid "77d0fe15-2bfd-57f0-991d-684ad2dfd05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399244} {:answer/id #uuid "a30c29de-8bbd-5ce5-b082-0d86e79ae9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399245} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de3ee5-4e26-5981-b4bd-fa52650fe448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399246} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0fd701-7b05-5e6d-9f51-cb1531e040d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399247} :answer-field/id #uuid "00acfb45-f915-59a3-94c8-f02ca6e1865b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "896ad0b2-d79e-5667-ae9b-69d97f290fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89417" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}_{3}-2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7cbd70ad-da38-5750-9d78-fe45b60fc21e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d27b92-6f03-55bd-b6a9-1d288bcf8c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454911} {:answer/id #uuid "2de3eabf-cab8-5591-a492-a96a3f773d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454970} {:answer/id #uuid "4d120a10-5675-5201-91f6-2be8ae41d759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455036} {:answer/id #uuid "5c95a6d3-17be-5589-ac03-00cd648b9d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455069} {:answer/id #uuid "9812912f-0bf4-5fe1-b7d3-e532d4e7dbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454911} :answer-field/id #uuid "061976f0-d2ab-56b5-ae57-17c4949d29c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b92ae563-4565-5947-89cb-f058a9c4a4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-37398" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "bd53a707-c6f5-5559-ae54-26e649e0c977" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290f4389-144e-58ae-8aa0-f8779961574c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355007} {:answer/id #uuid "426fd857-13b1-5ab3-9758-9722e35fc330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355251} {:answer/id #uuid "435fb70d-dab8-537c-a107-ef7870828b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355266} {:answer/id #uuid "994c1016-eda3-584d-9ca2-ccfb245592f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356025} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2df7b5-bcfb-56b5-bfec-d5975826e4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355266} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f55dc89-7192-5755-9048-0d366538fc46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "761e0b9c-f7fe-532d-9428-c3e7936605ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-89427" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +6)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-6.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-6)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "eb3f1758-bf5d-59f0-9e9b-84fea9836cfa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033fe036-222d-5d10-957e-6471d19dc5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321179} {:answer/id #uuid "2c482aed-cfdd-5bf4-acda-648b685f18fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321489} {:answer/id #uuid "2d64e163-f5c4-5413-9570-dd5493a5c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321500} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bd880a-2b37-5ebe-b42b-b4c304a5a933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322697} {:answer/id #uuid "e11d49f5-3c92-5fce-b032-36bf3f972d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322906}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322906} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e35bc9-d423-567e-8c0e-63b05f91b6ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4fce4c9f-341f-5617-8150-093fe040d196" :question/math-academy-id "q-37385" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "f596f2be-8e67-5ffa-bdef-b19f8d8da98f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476994}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476198}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539070} :activity/id #uuid "129a1854-2f4b-567e-bfbe-6c1b7fe4b689" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820 :topic/id #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" :topic/math-academy-id 1977} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2118b9d8-3a94-5241-ac9a-03c1a8165254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381113} {:answer/id #uuid "7efa2077-efeb-556a-bdf6-a527627aa068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381114} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe1eecd-6744-563e-a0ca-489b943e4e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381115} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5cac8f-2fc0-5759-807f-d9ee28d2044f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381116} {:answer/id #uuid "c81fa2d6-7df9-5381-b5a7-f75a91e5988d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381113} :answer-field/id #uuid "b84bb508-82f8-5a30-b1a6-92bf25dfe082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c980d806-f256-5388-b798-65b34624c78e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140722" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )[(3-\\lambda )^{2}-1] & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "54a37441-fb97-5482-8800-e7be9eb1f718"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13304c66-59ed-5804-b519-223c3709ae32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392040} {:answer/id #uuid "317f7bc8-17c3-5b65-8500-5bfb85e37bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392041} {:answer/id #uuid "808dfd35-c7cf-5560-bba9-dfef1206fd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392042} {:answer/id #uuid "94090702-def0-5eac-8760-9a3b321889de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392043} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb0fced-9501-56c9-8088-68d6df8fb853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392042} :answer-field/id #uuid "60b86f5a-a5c7-5a8b-9d41-513f5f26be40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad9f3c50-0a7e-56b3-becb-281cac541291" :question/math-academy-id "q-36973" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a lower triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =6.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -11 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-11{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "79acdebf-dccc-5a6c-84a3-c4482c1dbcac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26853257-a15d-56bd-baf3-05cb4af2b356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321448} {:answer/id #uuid "6215dc3a-949b-5a88-b400-1754de3451a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321914} {:answer/id #uuid "8f37a9ee-9183-5f94-b9e6-589f18d0723c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322284} {:answer/id #uuid "97007dd8-0a0c-5e5d-b2fe-2c703399817a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322352} {:answer/id #uuid "e831241f-8157-56bb-8b58-0b3139bec9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321448} :answer-field/id #uuid "001c22af-200a-5e74-a872-4fc6a4c58849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b75fe299-3a00-56ce-ac2b-3a6b8472e075" :question/math-academy-id "q-37401" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{12}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9de96552-9aff-59f8-bf73-97a5a9a29e02" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3279f5ec-b5ad-574c-aebf-693bd2fdaa1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269730} {:answer/id #uuid "653c963e-8cbf-558c-a076-0707a4bd7a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270171} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7e2990-b7e7-5be9-a37d-ee55358620c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270375} {:answer/id #uuid "81fcddb5-5383-56b8-bcf4-c993974f8e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270392} {:answer/id #uuid "ca767cd0-e2d5-5f15-b839-31ed41909f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271045}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186270392} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f091c3-7851-5215-bc6b-9b792d0722c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00737dc0-f397-573a-80e1-d038ce87150d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140717" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{2}+2(0) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "be19b29e-d4c3-5ad8-ba5e-8d63063a62a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539060}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88495c5e-727c-5bdc-8c66-f5f7a1da7068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399189} {:answer/id #uuid "973ba97a-cf4d-57f4-b6a7-6d7f845916bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399190} {:answer/id #uuid "a9de477f-3e83-5cb8-ab8c-3547e66a97cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399191} {:answer/id #uuid "89aba4dc-e9ba-5704-a200-86c4f6f35692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399192} {:answer/id #uuid "1965bab2-64b2-5353-bc34-d0701180dfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399189} :answer-field/id #uuid "adc9838b-0769-5bf4-b3d7-c8c2300681dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae635709-cef0-5d90-bcf8-39aaac1bc474" :question/math-academy-id "q-83480" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{9}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e095c51e-c500-590a-8101-841f07aafbc2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539096}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538218}]}
